  "image_digest": "sha256:0be6e16e2eb288dfd5fa8b0b41015f61731a139fb961d3366ccedf834289d781",
  "key_findings": [
    "Body size was the missing crossover variable: at rho~2, k0 speedup rises from below 1x at body512 to 1.70x at body1024 and 2.07x at body2048.",
    "Genuine k32 repair also crosses dense at long bodies: 1.53x at body1024 and 1.98x at body2048 under rho~2.",
    "For body1024/header64, k0 remains about 1.73x and k32 about 1.49-1.56x from target rho 0.9 through 3.0 despite increasing eviction volume.",
    "At body1024/rho~2, larger exact headers increase absolute TTFT for all modes but improve relative speedup: k0 grows from 1.69x at header0 to 1.76x at header256; k32 grows from 1.46x to 1.59x.",
    "Approximate recovery must evict exact Radix victims before allocator.alloc; bypassing that standard allocation sequence caused the first pressure crash.",
    "Long bodies require segmented canonical source registration on SM75; a single chunked approximate registration at body1024 exhausted allocator state, while two/four 512-token source segments restored 1024/2048 successfully."
  ],
  "known_limitations": [
    "Source bodies above 512 tokens are represented as multiple independently registered canonical segments; this is an implementation adaptation, not a single contiguous source-context KV object.",
    "k32 repairs only the leading 32 tokens of the overall restored run, not the first 32 tokens of every source segment.",
    "No accuracy or output-equivalence metric was collected.",
    "Pressure preparation time is excluded from target TTFT; the target allocation and any target-triggered eviction are included.",
    "Results are single-model SM75 measurements and require RTX PRO 6000/H100 replication."
  ],
